{ id:'opmcq-c12-1', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
  stem:'A **60-year-old male** patient complains of **sudden severe eye pain, redness and discharge**. History revealed that he **just had a cataract surgery 1 day ago**. Ophthalmoscopic examination revealed that the **red reflex was altered with a yellowish tint**. Externally, there was associated **lid edema, proptosis and corneal edema**. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Posterior vitreous detachment','Vitreous hemorrhage','Retinal detachment','Endophthalmitis','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**Three words in this stem settle it: *cataract surgery*, *pain*, and *yellowish*.**\n\n`L18) Cataract II` slide 16 prints post-operative endophthalmitis as a column of its own, and the stem is that column read back:\n\n> **Endophthalmitis** — *\"24–72 hrs (acute) or weeks (delayed)\"* · *\"Most serious vision-threatening complication.\"* · *\"Causative agents: CoNS (Staphylococcus epidermidis most common), S. aureus, streptococci.\"* · *\"Signs: **pain, redness, reduced VA, hypopyon, yellowish vitreous haze**.\"*\n\nOne day post-operatively is inside the deck's acute window; pain, redness and discharge are its signs; and the **yellowish reflex is the vitreous haze seen down an ophthalmoscope instead of at a slit lamp**.\n\n**Why a YELLOW reflex and not a black or a grey one.** `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slides 50–52 turn the red reflex into a four-way diagnostic key, and only one entry is yellow:\n\n| Reflex | What it means | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Black **shadows** in a red reflex | Media opacities — corneal scar, cataract, **vitreous haemorrhage** | `L3,4` slide 50 |\n| **Absent / black** | Mature cataract · dense vitreous haemorrhage | `L3,4` slide 51 |\n| **Grey** | **Retinal detachment** | `L3,4` slide 51 |\n| **Yellow / white** | Retinoblastoma · **\"Pus in the vitreous (endophthalmitis)\"** | `L3,4` slide 52 |\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.36 prints the same table in the book's own words: *\"**Pus in the vitreous cavity** (in **endophthalmitis**) will give a yellow reflex.\"* Pus scatters the returning light before it reaches the retina, so what comes back out is the creamy colour of the exudate; blood absorbs it, which is why a haemorrhage darkens rather than yellows the reflex.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — and note that all three are painless:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Posterior vitreous detachment** | Floaters and flashes in a **white, quiet, comfortable** eye. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 files it as an involutional change — *\"the vitreous humour degenerates and condenses (**vitreous syneresis**) ... called a posterior vitreous detachment\"* — not an infection, and it neither reddens the eye nor discharges. |\n| **Vitreous hemorrhage** | Painless, and the wrong colour: `L3,4` slide 51 gives a dense one an **absent (black) reflex**, and slide 50 gives a lesser one **black shadows**. This bank keys exactly that at `opmcq-c12-4` three questions later, in a patient with no pain at all. |\n| **Retinal detachment** | Painless, and its reflex is **grey** (`L3,4` slide 51). Its symptoms are flashes, floaters, a curtain and field loss — not discharge and lid oedema. |\n\n**⚠️ The lid oedema and proptosis are the stem's warning that this has gone beyond the eye.** The module runs that link the other way — `L10) orbit` slide 27 lists **endophthalmitis** among the ocular complications of bacterial orbital cellulitis — so orbital signs and intraocular pus keep company in this material. *(That an endophthalmitis spreading through the sclera into the orbit produces proptosis and lid oedema — panophthalmitis — is **not taken from the course material**; the module names the two conditions together but does not describe that progression.)*\n\n`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 135 puts **Endophthalmitis** on its list of ocular emergencies, which is what `opmcq-c12-2` and `opmcq-c12-3` go on to test.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Recognise acute post-operative endophthalmitis: pain, redness and reduced vision within 24–72 hours of intraocular surgery, with a yellowish reflex from pus in the vitreous — the one cause on the list that is painful and the one whose red reflex turns yellow rather than black or grey.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.80 (Ch.12 Q1, the first question under the "12. Vitreous" banner)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-2', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
  stem:'A **60-year-old man one day after cataract surgery** has **severe eye pain, redness and discharge**, a **red reflex altered with a yellowish tint**, and **lid oedema, proptosis and corneal oedema** — an acute post-operative **endophthalmitis**. Which of the following is the best next step to properly manage that patient?',
  options:['Surgery','Watchful waiting','Urgent referral to an ophthalmologist','Eye patching'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**This bank is written for the person who sees the patient first, not for the person who operates.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 135 lists **Endophthalmitis** among the **Ocular Emergencies**, alongside chemical burns, CRAO, acute angle-closure glaucoma and globe lacerations. An emergency on that list is defined by what the non-specialist must do about it: send it, now.\n\n**Why not simply \"surgery\".** The definitive treatment does exist and the module prints it — `L18) Cataract II` slide 16: *\"Tx: **urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)**. Severe: vitrectomy\"*, repeated on its revision slide 21 as *\"Endophthalmitis — intravitreal vancomycin + ceftazidime\"*. But **a tap-and-inject is an ophthalmologist's procedure in an ophthalmologist's theatre**, and \"Surgery\" as a bare option skips the step that gets the patient there. Note that Grade Gain asks this same question from the specialist's side and keys the drugs: `opqb-t12-510` keys *\"Immediate intravitreal antibiotics ± vitrectomy\"*. **Two banks, two audiences, two different correct answers — which is why these are two questions and not one.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Surgery** | Right destination, wrong step, and not yours to perform. It also mis-states the first-line treatment: the deck's own sequence is **tap and inject first**, with vitrectomy reserved for *\"Severe\"* disease. |\n| **B. Watchful waiting** | The single most damaging answer here. `L18` slide 16 calls endophthalmitis the *\"Most serious vision-threatening complication\"* of cataract surgery; the retina is being destroyed while the observation runs. |\n| **D. Eye patching** | Does nothing to an intraocular infection, and the module warns against reflex patching in an eye that may be compromised — `L25) Trauma I` slide 51 is emphatic that on a suspected open globe you *\"DO NOT PLACE A PATCH\"*. Patching an infected post-operative eye achieves only delay. |\n\n**⚠️ Back-reference repaired.** The source prints only *\"In the previous case, which of the following is the best next step to properly manage that patient?\"*; the antecedent is `opmcq-c12-1` on the same page, whose vignette has been restated here. The diagnosis is stated in the reconstructed stem because Q1 has already keyed it and this question tests the **disposition**, not the diagnosis.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Endophthalmitis is on the module\'s ocular-emergency list: the correct first move for a non-ophthalmologist is immediate referral, because the definitive treatment — vitreous tap plus intravitreal vancomycin and ceftazidime — is a specialist procedure that cannot wait.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.80 (Ch.12 Q2; printed as a bare back-reference, "In the previous case…", antecedent Q1. Four options only — the source prints no E)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-3', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
  stem:'In the **60-year-old man with acute post-operative endophthalmitis one day after cataract surgery**, which of the following is mandatory in all situations?',
  options:['Check IOP','CT orbit','Fortified antibiotics','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The question is asking which of the three is unconditional, and only one of them is.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 gives endophthalmitis exactly one treatment line and it contains no qualifier: *\"Tx: urgent vitreous tap + **intravitreal antibiotics** (vancomycin + ceftazidime). **Severe:** vitrectomy.\"* The word *Severe* is attached to the vitrectomy, not to the antibiotic — **every case gets the antibiotic; only some get the operation.** Slide 21 repeats the drugs on its own as the answer to *\"Most serious post-op complication?\"*.\n\n*(\"Fortified\" antibiotics — an antibiotic reconstituted at a concentration far above the commercial preparation — is a term the module does not print; `L15,16) Cornea` names the individual agents for bacterial keratitis but never the word \"fortified\". **Not taken from the course material.** The bank's option is nevertheless the antibiotic option, and it is the only one of the three that the module makes unconditional.)*\n\n**Why the other two are conditional, and therefore not \"mandatory in all situations\":**\n\n| Option | When it is indicated — and why not always |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Check IOP** | Intraocular pressure matters in this material where the pressure itself is the disease (`L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG`) or where a lens is blocking the angle (`L12` on phacomorphic and phacolytic glaucoma). It is not part of the endophthalmitis protocol, and `L25) Trauma I` slide 51 shows the module is willing to **forbid** it outright in an eye whose integrity is in doubt — *\"DO NOT CHECK EYE PRESSURE\"* on a suspected open globe. A test the module sometimes forbids cannot be mandatory in all situations. |\n| **B. CT orbit** | An imaging study for **orbital** questions and for foreign bodies. `L25) Trauma I` slide 52 reserves CT for a suspected intraocular foreign body — *\"Determine the location and presence of a metallic IOFB\"* — and `L10) orbit` uses it for cellulitis and orbital masses. In a straightforward post-operative endophthalmitis the diagnosis is clinical and the scan only costs time. |\n| **D / E. All / None of the above** | Both fail on the same logic: D would require A and B to be unconditional, which they are not, and E would require the antibiotic to be dispensable, which the deck's own treatment line makes it not. |\n\n**⚠️ Back-reference repaired, and the antecedent is NOT the previous number.** The source prints *\"In the previous case…\"*; `opmcq-c12-2` is itself a back-reference, so the chain runs Q3 → Q2 → **Q1**, the 60-year-old with post-operative endophthalmitis. Read at face value, \"the previous case\" would point at a question with no case in it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Antibiotic cover is unconditional in endophthalmitis; tonometry and CT are situational, and the module even forbids checking IOP in an eye that may be open.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.80 (Ch.12 Q3; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q1 through Q2)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-4', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
  stem:"A **56-year-old diabetic male** patient presents complaining of **acutely diminished vision**. He reported that **before** that drop in visual acuity, he noticed **multiple floaters and flashes of light**. Ophthalmoscopy was performed and it revealed **a number of black shadows obvious against the red reflex and hence obscuring the retina**. Which of the following conditions is the most likely diagnosis of the patient's condition?",
  options:['Retinal detachment','CRVO','Posterior vitreous detachment','Vitreous hemorrhage','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**\"Black shadows obvious against the red reflex\" is a quotation, and it has exactly one owner in this material.** `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 50:\n\n> *\"**Opacities in the media** (e.g., corneal scar, cataract, **vitreous haemorrhage**) appear as **black shadows** and can be best seen when the pupil has been dilated.\"*\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.36 prints the identical sentence. Blood suspended in the gel absorbs the examiner's light on the way in and on the way back, so wherever a clot sits the returning glow is missing — a dark silhouette against whatever red reflex survives around it. As the haemorrhage thickens the shadows merge, and `L3,4` slide 51 names that end state **\"Dense vitreous hemorrhage (Black reflex)\"**.\n\n**Why a diabetic bleeds into his vitreous.** `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prints the indications for vitreoretinal surgery and heads the list with **\"Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage\"**, beside *\"Dense, persistent premacular haemorrhage\"* — the deck records the bleed as the standard endpoint of proliferative disease even though it never narrates the rupture in prose. Its treatment slides show why: proliferative diabetic retinopathy grows **NVD** and **NVE**, new vessels graded by disc area, and those vessels sit on a vitreous that moves with every eye movement *(that the new vessels are fragile and tear when the gel tugs on them is **not taken from the course material**)*.\n\n**Why the floaters and flashes came FIRST, and are not the diagnosis.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists **PVD** and **retinal tear / detachment** as the causes of photopsia, and p.42 lists **PVD, vitreous haemorrhage** and **retinal tear / detachment** as the causes of floaters. In this patient the vitreous separated, tugged, and then the bleed followed — which is why the stem is careful to put the floaters *before* the drop in acuity. The bank tests exactly that sequence again at `opmcq-c12-18`.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Retinal detachment** | The reflex is wrong. `L3,4` slide 51 gives a detachment a **grey** reflex, not black shadows, and a detachment does not obscure the retina — it displaces it. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 6 makes the fluid, not blood, the pathology. |\n| **B. CRVO** | A vein occlusion floods the retina with **flame and blot haemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins and disc swelling** — all of which are seen **on** the retina, so the fundus view must be clear enough to see them. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives central retinal vein occlusion a *\"Very poor prognosis\"* line but no media opacity. If the fundus is obscured, you are not looking at a CRVO. |\n| **C. Posterior vitreous detachment** | ⚠️ **The trap, and it is a good one, because the stem's opening symptoms are a PVD.** But `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 describes PVD as an **involutional** change that *\"can predispose the patient to retinal traction, retinal tears\"* — it does not itself opacify the media. A PVD leaves acuity **normal** (Grade Gain's own vignette at `opqb-t12-502` says so explicitly). The moment the vision drops acutely and the retina disappears behind shadows, something opaque has been added to the gel. |\n\n**⚠️ Grade Gain asks this in the opposite direction and keys a different word.** `opqb-t12-505` gives the same diabetic with an obscured fundus and keys **Proliferative diabetic retinopathy** — the *disease*; House keys **Vitreous hemorrhage** — the *finding*. Neither key appears on the other's option list. And `opqb-t12-506` keys **\"Black shadows against red reflex\"** as its answer, where this stem prints it as a given.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-acute` — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 heads its **Acute painless loss of vision** box with **\"1. Vitreous haemorrhage\"**, and `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 133 puts **Vitreous Haemorrhage** in the *Acute* column of its loss-of-vision table. Filed under diabetic retinopathy because the diabetes is what supplies the mechanism.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Black shadows against the red reflex obscuring the retina are the module\'s signature of a vitreous haemorrhage; in a diabetic, the bleed comes from the new vessels of proliferative retinopathy, and preceding floaters and flashes mark the vitreous separation that set it off.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.80-81 (Ch.12 Q4). ⚠️ ORPHANED ACROSS THE PAGE BREAK: stem and options A-C at the foot of p.80; options D and E and the answer line at the head of p.81. Source prints "presents to the ophthalmologist\'s complaining" and "acutely-diminished"; both normalised' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-5', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
  stem:'In the **56-year-old diabetic with acute visual loss, preceded by floaters and flashes, whose ophthalmoscopy showed black shadows against the red reflex obscuring the retina**, which of the following is the best next step to properly evaluate that condition?',
  options:['Ultrasound','MRI','CT orbit','Dilated fundus examination','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"**When you cannot see through the media, you image through them.** Blood in the gel blocks light but not sound, so B-scan ultrasound draws the posterior segment the ophthalmoscope has lost — and the specific question it answers is whether there is a **retinal detachment or a tear behind the blood**, which changes the operation and its urgency.\n\nThe module uses ultrasound for exactly this purpose. `L25) Trauma I` slide 52, working up an eye whose view is compromised, lists *\"**Ultrasound may be useful**\"* among the investigations, and slides 53/54 print a **B-scan ultrasound of an IOFB** beside the fundus photograph. `L33,34) The White Eye` reaches for the same modality in an eye with an opaque media. *(That the specific indication in a vitreous haemorrhage is to exclude an underlying retinal detachment is **not taken from the course material** — no cached ophthalmology deck prints a B-scan protocol for vitreous haemorrhage; `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` records in its own not-covered list that it contains **\"ANY INVESTIGATION: no B-scan ultrasound, no OCT, no fundus fluorescein angiography\"**. Grade Gain states it outright: `opqb-t12-507` keys **B-scan ultrasound** as the investigation *\"essential to rule out retinal detachment\"* in this exact clinical situation.)*\n\n**Why the other four are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **B. MRI** | Slow, expensive, poor for the posterior segment, and the module prints a **prohibition** on it in the neighbouring situation: `L25) Trauma I` slide 52, *\"**DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED**\"*, with slide 53/54 captioned *\"Metallic IOFB ??? → CT ✓ · MRI ✗\"*. A diabetic vitreous haemorrhage is not a magnet hazard, but MRI still answers no question here that ultrasound does not answer faster. |\n| **C. CT orbit** | Images bone and orbital soft tissue. `L25` slide 52 gives its two jobs — locate a metallic foreign body and *\"Show the shape and integrity of the traumatized globe\"* — neither of which is being asked. CT cannot resolve whether a retina is attached. |\n| **D. Dilated fundus examination** | ⚠️ **The option that would be right in almost any other question in this chapter, and is wrong here for one reason: it has already failed.** The stem says the shadows are *obscuring the retina*. Dilating a pupil widens the window; it does not clear what is behind it. The bank keys this very option at `opmcq-c12-8` and `opmcq-c12-11` — in patients whose media let light through. |\n| **E. None of the above** | Excluded by A. |\n\n**⚠️ This question and `opmcq-c12-8` print the SAME FIVE OPTIONS IN THE SAME ORDER and key different letters — A here, D there.** The option menu is a template this bank re-uses; what discriminates is the antecedent. Here the fundus is obscured, so the answer is the modality that sees through blood; there the media are clear, so the answer is to look. **A key letter is never the thing to memorise.**\n\n**⚠️ Back-reference repaired:** the source prints only *\"In the previous case…\"*; the antecedent is `opmcq-c12-4` on the facing page.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A vitreous haemorrhage dense enough to obscure the fundus is investigated by B-scan ultrasound, because sound passes where light cannot and the question to be answered is whether the retina is detached behind the blood.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.81 (Ch.12 Q5; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q4. Option list identical to Q8\'s, keyed differently)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-6', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
  stem:'In the **56-year-old diabetic whose ophthalmoscopy showed black shadows against the red reflex obscuring the retina**, which of the following is the best line of management for the patient’s symptoms?',
  options:['Follow up in non-urgent cases','Surgery','No specific treatment','A & B','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**The key is \"A & B\" because the module's own indication is written as a condition, not a command.** `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prints four *Indications for vitreoretinal surgery* and the first of them is:\n\n> **\"Severe **persistent** vitreous haemorrhage\"** — beside *\"Dense, **persistent** premacular haemorrhage\"*, *\"Progressive proliferation **despite laser therapy**\"* and *\"Retinal detachment **involving macula**\"*.\n\nEvery one of the four carries a qualifier. **Blood in the vitreous is given time to clear on its own; it is the failure to clear that brings the patient to theatre.** So the complete management is both halves of the option: **watch the ones that are not urgent, operate on the ones that are** — which is precisely *A & B*.\n\n**What the two halves mean in practice:**\n\n| Half | What it is | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Follow up in non-urgent cases** | A fresh, non-clearing-yet haemorrhage in an eye with no detachment. The deck's word is **\"persistent\"** — an interval must elapse before the haemorrhage qualifies. | `L19.1`, indications for vitreoretinal surgery |\n| **B. Surgery** | Pars plana vitrectomy, once the haemorrhage is severe and persistent, or once traction or a macula-involving detachment appears. | `L19.1`, same list; Grade Gain keys the same idea at `opqb-t12-499` (*\"Non-resolving vitreous hemorrhage\"*) and excludes central serous retinopathy at `opqb-t12-498` |\n\n**Why the other options fail:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A alone** | Correct for the mild case and catastrophic for the other one. Following up a **macula-involving tractional detachment** is watching a retina die; `L19.1` puts it on the surgical list for that reason. |\n| **B alone** | Over-treats. A vitrectomy carried out on a haemorrhage that would have absorbed exposes the eye to the risks of intraocular surgery — including, as this very chapter opens with, **endophthalmitis** (`opmcq-c12-1`). |\n| **C. No specific treatment** | True of an **uncomplicated posterior vitreous detachment**, which this bank keys at `opmcq-c12-9` and `opmcq-c12-20`. It is not true of a diabetic vitreous haemorrhage, which has a named surgical indication in the deck. ⚠️ **This is the option that punishes reading the chapter as one topic instead of two diseases.** |\n| **E. None of the above** | Excluded by D. |\n\n**⚠️ Back-reference repaired:** the source prints *\"In the previous case…\"*, and the chain runs Q6 → Q5 → **Q4**. The source also prints a **double space** — *\"the best line of  management\"* — verified at 2× on the 200 dpi render; normalised here.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Vitreous haemorrhage in diabetes is managed by observation OR vitrectomy depending on persistence and complications — the deck\'s surgical indication is qualified by the words "severe persistent", so neither watching alone nor operating alone is the whole answer.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.81 (Ch.12 Q6; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q4 through Q5. Source prints a double space in "line of  management")' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-7', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'A **22-year-old male** patient presented to the ophthalmology clinic with a complaint of **seeing bugs or cobwebs together with light flashes**. Ophthalmoscopy revealed an **evident Weiss ring**. Which of the following might occur as a complication to that patient’s condition?',
  options:['Endophthalmitis','Cataract','Retinal tears','Glaucoma'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The stem describes a posterior vitreous detachment, and the book names its complication in the same sentence in which it defines it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17:\n\n> *\"With age the vitreous humour degenerates and condenses (**vitreous syneresis**) and may separate from the retina — called a **posterior vitreous detachment**. This ... **can predispose the patient to retinal traction, retinal tears** and subsequent retinal detachment.\"*\n\nThat is the whole mechanism: the gel shrinks, pulls away, and **where it is still stuck it pulls a hole in the retina.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` completes the sequence — a **rhegmatogenous** detachment is one that begins at a retinal break, through which liquefied vitreous passes into the subretinal space.\n\n**The two symptoms are the book's own, and they map onto the two halves of the event:**\n\n| Symptom | What produces it | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Flashes (photopsia)** | Traction on the retina; the retina answers any stimulus with light. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists the causes of photopsia as **PVD**, **retinal tear / detachment** and migraine. | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 |\n| **Floaters — \"bugs or cobwebs\"** | Condensed collagen strands casting shadows. p.42 lists the causes of floaters as **PVD**, **vitreous haemorrhage** and **retinal tear / detachment**. | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 |\n\n⚠️ **Flashes and floaters together are the module's warning pair, not a reassurance pair** — three of the four conditions on those two lists are the same three, and one of them blinds.\n\n*(The **Weiss ring** — the detached ring of condensed glial tissue that lifts off the optic disc margin and is seen floating in front of it, the single most specific sign that a PVD has completed — is **not taken from the course material**. \"Weiss\" returns exactly one case-insensitive hit across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 book chapter caches, and that hit is inside `L20`'s own list of things it does **not** cover. Grade Gain does print the eponym, keying it at `opqb-t12-503`.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Endophthalmitis** | An **infection**, and this bank has just spent three questions establishing where it comes from — `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 attaches it to intraocular surgery, and `L25) Trauma I` to a penetrating injury (this chapter keys that at `opmcq-c12-19`). A vitreous that separates does not become infected; nothing has breached the eye. |\n| **B. Cataract** | A lens opacity. `L17/L18` give its causes as age, diabetes, steroids, trauma and uveitis — the vitreous is not among them, and a PVD does not touch the lens. |\n| **D. Glaucoma** | An optic neuropathy driven by aqueous dynamics at the **anterior** segment. `L11) Glaucoma Diag, POAG` locates the obstruction at the trabecular meshwork and `L12` at the angle; a posterior vitreous separation alters neither. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A posterior vitreous detachment — floaters, flashes and a Weiss ring — threatens the retina, not the lens or the pressure: where the gel stays adherent it pulls, and traction tears the retina.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.81 (Ch.12 Q7). Four options only — the source prints no E. Antecedent for Q8, Q9 and, with one added sign, Q11' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-8', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'In the **22-year-old seeing bugs or cobwebs with light flashes, whose ophthalmoscopy revealed an evident Weiss ring**, which of the following is the best next step to properly evaluate that condition?',
  options:['Ultrasound','MRI','CT orbit','Dilated fundus examination','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**Because the media are clear, you look — and you look at the periphery, because that is where the tear will be.** The point of the examination is not to confirm the PVD, which the Weiss ring has already done; it is to find the complication the book warns of at `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17, *\"retinal traction, **retinal tears** and subsequent retinal detachment\"*, while it is still a treatable hole rather than a detachment.\n\nDilation is what makes that possible. `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 50 states the principle for any fundus view — media opacities *\"can be best seen when the **pupil has been dilated**\"* — and an undilated pupil simply cannot deliver the far peripheral retina where breaks occur. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` builds its whole account on peripheral breaks and degenerations.\n\n**⚠️ THIS QUESTION PRINTS THE SAME FIVE OPTIONS IN THE SAME ORDER AS `opmcq-c12-5`, AND THE KEY IS A DIFFERENT LETTER — D here, A there.** The discriminating token is not in the option list at all; it is in the antecedent:\n\n| | `opmcq-c12-5` | `opmcq-c12-8` (this one) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Antecedent | Q4 — diabetic, **fundus obscured** by black shadows | Q7 — **Weiss ring visible on ophthalmoscopy** |\n| State of the media | Opaque with blood | **Clear** — you can see the disc |\n| Therefore | Image **through** the blood: **Ultrasound** | **Look**: dilated fundus examination |\n\n**A shared option menu is how this bank pairs questions, not evidence that they are one question.** Reading the letter instead of the antecedent gets one of the two wrong every time.\n\n**Why the other options fail here:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Ultrasound** | Reserved for the eye you cannot see into — `L25) Trauma I` slide 52, *\"Ultrasound may be useful\"* in a compromised view. Here the view is open, and B-scan resolves the peripheral retina far worse than a dilated examination does. |\n| **B. MRI** | No role, and `L25` slide 52 prints an outright prohibition in the adjacent scenario: *\"DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED.\"* |\n| **C. CT orbit** | Images bone and orbital soft tissue; `L25` slide 52 gives its indications as locating a metallic foreign body and assessing globe integrity. It cannot show a retinal break. |\n| **E. None of the above** | Excluded by D. |\n\n**⚠️ Back-reference repaired:** the source prints only *\"In the previous case…\"*, antecedent `opmcq-c12-7`.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A symptomatic posterior vitreous detachment is evaluated by dilated fundus examination to find a retinal tear while the media are still clear — the same option list as the vitreous-haemorrhage question, keyed differently because there the fundus cannot be seen.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.81-82 (Ch.12 Q8). ⚠️ ORPHANED ACROSS THE PAGE BREAK: stem and all five options at the foot of p.81, the answer line "Answer: D" standing alone at the head of p.82. Printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q7. Option list identical to Q5\'s, keyed differently' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-9', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'In the **22-year-old seeing bugs or cobwebs with light flashes, whose ophthalmoscopy revealed an evident Weiss ring**, which of the following is the best line of management for the patient’s symptoms?',
  options:['Steroids','Surgery','Antibiotics','No specific treatment','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**A posterior vitreous detachment is an involutional change, and the module does not treat involutional changes — it watches them.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 introduces it in exactly those terms: *\"With age the vitreous humour degenerates and condenses (vitreous syneresis) and may separate from the retina.\"* The book gives no drug and no operation, because there is nothing to reverse: **the gel does not reattach.** What matters is not the separation but whether it took a piece of retina with it, which `opmcq-c12-8` has just excluded by dilated examination.\n\nThe floaters themselves settle. *(That symptomatic floaters typically become less noticeable over weeks to months as the opacities settle inferiorly and the patient's attention adapts is **not taken from the course material** — no cached deck prints a natural history for PVD floaters.)* **What the patient is given instead of treatment is instructions:** to return immediately for a **sudden shower of new floaters, a marked increase in flashes, or a curtain across the field**, because those are the symptoms of the retinal tear or detachment that p.17 warns the PVD predisposes to. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` is built on that presentation.\n\n**Why each treatment option is wrong — and note that each one is the correct answer to a different question in this same chapter:**\n\n| Option | Belongs to | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Steroids** | Inflammation — the uveitides of `L21,22) Uveitis`. A PVD is not inflammatory; steroids treat nothing here and carry their own cataract and pressure risk (`L17/L18` list steroids among cataractogenic causes). | `L21,22` |\n| **B. Surgery** | The **complicated** vitreous: `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` lists *\"Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage\"* and *\"Retinal detachment involving macula\"* among the indications for vitreoretinal surgery — this bank keys that at `opmcq-c12-6`. Operating on an uncomplicated PVD adds the risks of intraocular surgery, endophthalmitis included, for no gain. | `L19.1` |\n| **C. Antibiotics** | **Endophthalmitis** — `L18) Cataract II` slide 16, *\"urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)\"*; this chapter keys it at `opmcq-c12-3`. Nothing here is infected. | `L18` sl.16 |\n| **E. None of the above** | Excluded by D. |\n\n**⚠️ The bank asks this twice.** `opmcq-c12-20` puts the same judgement as a direct question — *\"uncomplicated posterior vitreous detachment with **no retinal tears or hemorrhage**\"* — and keys **No treatment** against a different option list (immediate laser, vitrectomy, intravitreal antibiotics). The discriminating token is that Q20 states the absence of complications in its own stem, where this question inherits it from the chain.\n\n**⚠️ Back-reference repaired:** the source prints *\"In the previous case…\"*, and the chain runs Q9 → Q8 → **Q7**.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'An uncomplicated posterior vitreous detachment needs no steroid, antibiotic or operation — only exclusion of a retinal break and warning symptoms to return on.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.82 (Ch.12 Q9; printed as "In the previous case…", antecedent Q7 through Q8)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-10', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-intro',
  stem:'Which of the following accurately describes **vitreous humor**?',
  options:["It's a clear watery liquid","It's a turbid gel","It's a clear gel","Its water content is low",'None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**Two properties, and the question splits the field on both: it is a GEL, and it is CLEAR.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.8 states it directly in the anatomy of the posterior segment:\n\n> *\"The **vitreous humour** is a **transparent gel-like**, **avascular** structure that fills the posterior cavity of the eye, occupying about **two-thirds of the volume** of the eye. It is about **98% water**.\"*\n\n`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slides 37–39 print the same anatomy in the deck's own words.\n\n**Why 98 % water and still a gel — the property the question is actually testing.** The remaining ~2 % is a scaffold of **collagen fibrils** with **hyaluronic acid** holding water within it; a very small amount of structure suspends a very large amount of water. That is also why the gel can fail in the way the rest of this chapter depends on: when the network breaks down with age (**syneresis**, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17) the gel liquefies in pockets, collapses, and separates from the retina.\n\n**Why the other options are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. A clear watery liquid** | Clear, yes — but this describes the **aqueous humour**, not the vitreous. The two are different structures with different jobs: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.8 and `L1,2` put the aqueous in the **anterior** segment, secreted by the ciliary body and drained through the trabecular meshwork (which is why aqueous dynamics produce glaucoma and vitreous dynamics do not). ⚠️ **The distractor that catches everyone who remembers only \"98 % water\".** |\n| **B. A turbid gel** | Right consistency, wrong optics. **Turbidity is pathology, not anatomy.** A vitreous that is not clear is a vitreous with something in it, and `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slides 50–52 catalogue what: blood gives **black shadows** or an absent reflex (`opmcq-c12-4`), pus gives a **yellow** reflex (`opmcq-c12-1`). |\n| **D. Its water content is low** | The exact inverse of the book. p.8: *\"about **98% water**.\"* |\n| **E. None of the above** | Excluded by C. |\n\n*(Secondary chapter: this is anatomy of the posterior segment and sits with the vitreous pathology it underpins; filed to the module's introductory anatomy chapter because that is where `ophthalmology.pdf` p.8 and `L1,2` slides 37–39 are taught.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'The vitreous humour is a transparent, avascular GEL — 98% water held in a collagen and hyaluronic-acid scaffold, filling two-thirds of the eye. Clear-but-watery describes aqueous; turbid describes disease.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.82 (Ch.12 Q10; the only pure-anatomy question in the chapter)' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-11', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'A **22-year-old male** patient presented complaining of **seeing bugs or cobwebs together with light flashes**. Ophthalmoscopy revealed an **evident Weiss ring and absent red reflex**. Which of the following is the best next step in the management of that patient?',
  options:['Reassurance and follow-up in 1 month','Optical coherence tomography (OCT)','CT scan of the orbit','Dilated fundus examination'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**This is `opmcq-c12-7`'s vignette with four words added — *\"and absent red reflex\"* — and those four words are the entire question.** A PVD on its own leaves the media clear; the moment the reflex goes, something has entered the gel. `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 51 names what:\n\n> **Absent (black) red reflex** — *\"Mature cataract · **Dense vitreous haemorrhage**.\"*\n\nIn a 22-year-old with a Weiss ring and no cataract history, the loss of the reflex means **the PVD has bled** — the separating gel has torn a retinal vessel, which is exactly the complication `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 predicts (*\"retinal traction, retinal tears and subsequent retinal detachment\"*) and p.42 lists as a cause of floaters. **A tear that bleeds is a tear, and a tear untreated becomes a detachment.** So the question is no longer whether to reassure; it is how urgently to find the break.\n\n**Why D and not A — the reason this pair exists.** `opmcq-c12-9` keys *No specific treatment* for the identical patient **without** the reflex change. Add the reflex change and reassurance becomes the dangerous answer. **The discriminating token is \"absent red reflex\" and nothing else in the two stems differs.**\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Reassurance and follow-up in 1 month** | Correct for an **uncomplicated** PVD (`opmcq-c12-9`, `opmcq-c12-20`), and wrong here: a month is long enough for a retinal break to become a macula-off detachment, which `L19.1` lists among the indications for surgery and `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` treats as the emergency it is. |\n| **B. OCT** | Images the **macula** in cross-section at high resolution — the modality of `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy`'s macular oedema and of the posterior pole generally. It does not survey the **peripheral** retina where breaks occur, and it needs a clear media to acquire. |\n| **C. CT scan of the orbit** | `L25) Trauma I` slide 52 gives CT its two indications — locate a metallic intraocular foreign body and *\"Show the shape and integrity of the traumatized globe\"*. There is no trauma in this stem and CT cannot show a retinal break. |\n\n**⚠️ Recorded, not disputed — a note on the option list.** With the reflex **absent**, the module's own instrument for an eye you cannot see into is **B-scan ultrasound** (`L25` slide 52, *\"Ultrasound may be useful\"*), which this bank itself keys at `opmcq-c12-5`. **Ultrasound is not on this question's list.** Of the four printed, dilated fundus examination is the only one that looks for the break at all, and dilation is what recovers a view through a partial opacity (`L3,4` slide 50, opacities *\"can be best seen when the pupil has been dilated\"*). The key is correct **as printed against the options printed**; the observation is that a fifth option would have beaten it. `answer` is the bank's key.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Adding "absent red reflex" to a posterior vitreous detachment converts it from a reassurance case into a search for a retinal break, because a lost reflex in a young eye means the gel has bled.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.82 (Ch.12 Q11; Q7\'s vignette restated in full with "and absent red reflex" added — not a back-reference). Four options only — the source prints no E' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-12', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
  stem:'In **a child with vitreous hemorrhage**, which of the following is true regarding management?',
  options:['Manage exactly similar to an adult','Manage similar to an adult with caution to rule out child abuse','Manage in a different way than an adult','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**The key is the option that keeps the ophthalmology and adds the safeguarding — \"similar to an adult\", *with* a caution.** The two halves have to be read together, because A and C each keep only one of them.\n\n**The ophthalmic half.** The eye is managed on the same principles at any age: establish why it bled, image through the blood when the view is lost (`L25) Trauma I` slide 52, *\"Ultrasound may be useful\"*), and operate on the haemorrhage that will not clear or that hides a detachment (`L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy`'s vitreoretinal indications — *\"Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage\"*, *\"Retinal detachment involving macula\"*). Nothing about being a child changes that logic.\n\n**The safeguarding half — and why it is the whole point of the question.** The adult causes of a vitreous haemorrhage largely do not exist in a child: **proliferative diabetic retinopathy** (`L19.1`) takes years of disease to develop, **retinal vein occlusion** (`L19.2`) is a disease of vascular ageing, and age-related **PVD** with syneresis is by definition involutional (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.17). Subtract those and **trauma** dominates — which is why an unexplained vitreous haemorrhage in a young child raises the question of an inflicted injury.\n\n*(**Not taken from the course material**, and measured: *\"child abuse\"* and *\"non-accidental\"* return **zero** case-insensitive hits across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 book chapter caches — the only \"abuse\" hit in the module is *\"topical anaesthetic abuse\"* in `L15,16) Cornea`, an unrelated corneal toxicity. The specific association of retinal and vitreous haemorrhage with abusive head trauma in infants, and the obligation to examine both eyes and involve child protection, comes from outside the module.)*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Manage exactly similar to an adult** | Right about the eye, and it is the word **\"exactly\"** that fails: it discards the safeguarding step, and the child leaves with a treated eye and an unexamined cause. |\n| **C. Manage in a different way than an adult** | Overstates. The vitrectomy, the ultrasound and the observation period are the same procedures; what is added is a question about aetiology, not a different treatment. |\n| **D. None of the above** | Excluded by B. |\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-acute` — a vitreous haemorrhage is the first entry in `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41's acute painless visual loss box, and `L1,2` slide 133 files it in the *Acute* column. Filed under trauma because the key turns entirely on inflicted injury.)*\n\n**⚠️ One further reason a child is not simply a small adult, which the option does not mention:** a dense vitreous haemorrhage occludes the visual axis, and in the amblyopic age range that risks **deprivation amblyopia** — the module's own principle at `L26/L27,28) Squint & Amblyopia`, where any obstruction of the visual axis in early childhood demands prompt clearing. *(The application to vitreous haemorrhage specifically is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'A child with a vitreous haemorrhage gets the same ophthalmic management as an adult plus a deliberate search for the cause: the adult causes — diabetic retinopathy, vein occlusion, involutional PVD — do not apply, so trauma, including inflicted injury, must be excluded.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.82 (Ch.12 Q12). Four options only — the source prints D = "None of the above" and no E' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-13', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most important prognostic factor** for a patient presenting with **endophthalmitis**?',
  options:['Duration of the condition','Severity of pain','Visual acuity','Associated comorbidities','All of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The presenting visual acuity is the measurement everything else is judged by — it grades the disease, it selects the operation, and it predicts the outcome.**\n\n`L18) Cataract II` slide 16 makes vision the axis on which the treatment turns: *\"Tx: urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime). **Severe: vitrectomy.**\"* **\"Severe\" in an infected eye is measured in vision, not in symptoms**, and this bank spells out where the line falls two questions later — `opmcq-c12-15` keys **PL or worse vision** as the threshold for admission and immediate vitrectomy. A factor that decides whether the eye goes to theatre is by definition the prognostic factor.\n\nThe module supplies the scale. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.20 print the descending ladder: counting fingers → **hand movements (HM)** → **perception of light (PL)** → **no perception of light (NoPL)**. An eye still reading letters has a retina that light is reaching; an eye down to PL has a vitreous full of pus and a retina already under toxic assault.\n\n*(That presenting visual acuity is the strongest single predictor of final vision in endophthalmitis, and the basis on which immediate vitrectomy is selected, is **not taken from the course material** — the trial literature behind that threshold returns zero case-insensitive hits across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 book chapter caches. What the module does print is the treatment split at *\"Severe\"*, and this bank's own threshold at `opmcq-c12-15`.)*\n\n**Why the other options are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Duration of the condition** | Matters — `L18` slide 16 divides acute (*\"24–72 hrs\"*) from delayed (*\"weeks\"*) presentation, and delay is bad. But duration is a **proxy**: what the delay has done to the eye is read off the acuity, which measures the damage directly instead of estimating it. |\n| **B. Severity of pain** | ⚠️ **The trap for anyone grading disease by how loudly it presents.** Pain is a symptom of inflammation, not of retinal destruction, and it can be severe in an eye that recovers and modest in an eye that does not. Note that the module's most sight-destroying vitreous conditions — haemorrhage, PVD, detachment — are **painless** throughout (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.41, acute **painless** loss of vision). |\n| **D. Associated comorbidities** | Relevant to susceptibility and to healing, and to the endogenous route in an immunocompromised or septic patient — but the eye's fate is decided by how much of it the infection has already taken. |\n| **E. All of the above** | The question asks for the **most important** one, which forbids a collective answer. |\n\n**⚠️ Back-reference note:** this is **not** a back-reference — it opens a fresh endophthalmitis run (Q13, Q15, Q17) after the PVD questions, and the vignette of `opmcq-c12-1` is not carried forward.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Presenting visual acuity is the prognostic factor in endophthalmitis: it grades severity, sets the threshold for immediate vitrectomy, and predicts the final result — pain does not.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.82-83 (Ch.12 Q13). ⚠️ ORPHANED ACROSS THE PAGE BREAK: the stem stands alone at the foot of p.82 with no options under it; the entire option list A-E and the answer line are at the head of p.83' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-14', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'All of the following are among the findings in a patient with **posterior vitreous detachment** **except**?',
  options:['Vossious ring','Weiss ring','Flashes','Floaters','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"**The question is built on two rings that sound alike, sit in different parts of the eye, and are caused by different things.**\n\n| | **Vossious ring** (option A — the exception) | **Weiss ring** (option B) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Where | On the **anterior lens capsule** | In the **vitreous**, in front of the optic disc |\n| What it is | A ring of **iris pigment** imprinted on the lens | A ring of condensed glial tissue torn off the disc margin |\n| Cause | **Blunt ocular trauma** | **Posterior vitreous detachment** |\n| Module source | `L25) Trauma I` **slides 30/31**, *\"Blunt trauma: Vossious ring\"* — *\"pigment ring on the anterior lens capsule\"*; `L18) Cataract II` lists *\"Vossius ring (blunt)\"* among the signs of traumatic cataract | Not printed in the module (see below) |\n\nThe mechanism is the module's own: blunt trauma drives the iris back against the lens, and the pigment epithelium leaves its print where the pupil margin struck. `L25) Trauma I` files it with the other blunt-injury signs — hyphaema, iridodialysis, angle recession — none of which involve the vitreous at all.\n\n**⚠️ SPELLING — AND THE MODULE ITSELF PRINTS IT BOTH WAYS.** House spells it **\"Vossious\"**, which matches `L25) Trauma I` slide 30/31 exactly; `L18) Cataract II` spells it **\"Vossius\"**, which is the standard form of the eponym. **Two spellings inside one module for one sign** — recorded, not corrected. A grep for either spelling alone finds only half the module.\n\n**Why the other three ARE findings in a PVD:**\n\n| Option | Source |\n|---|---|\n| **B. Weiss ring** | The sign that a PVD has completed. *(**Not taken from the course material** — \"Weiss\" returns exactly one case-insensitive hit across all 29 cached decks and all 12 book chapter caches, and that hit is inside `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations`'s own list of what it does **not** cover. Grade Gain prints the eponym and keys it at `opqb-t12-503`.)* This bank puts it in the stem of `opmcq-c12-7` and `opmcq-c12-11`. |\n| **C. Flashes** | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists the causes of **photopsia** as PVD, retinal tear / detachment and migraine — traction on the retina, which answers any stimulus with light. |\n| **D. Floaters** | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 lists the causes of floaters as **PVD**, vitreous haemorrhage and retinal tear / detachment — condensed collagen casting shadows. |\n| **E. None of the above** | Would assert that all four are PVD findings; A is not. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Distinguish the Vossius ring — iris pigment on the anterior lens capsule after BLUNT TRAUMA — from the Weiss ring, flashes and floaters, which are the findings of a posterior vitreous detachment.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.83 (Ch.12 Q14). ⚠️ The source spells option A "Vossious ring", matching L25) Trauma I sl.30/31; L18) Cataract II spells the same sign "Vossius ring". Spelling preserved as printed' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-15', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
  stem:'Which of the following is an indication to **admit** a patient with **endophthalmitis** to hospital and perform **immediate vitrectomy**?',
  options:["Admit all cases of endophthalmitis as it's an ocular emergency",'HM or worse vision','PL or better vision','HM or better vision','PL or worse vision'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"**This is a threshold question, and the whole of it lives in the direction of the inequality.** `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.20 print the descending acuity ladder — counting fingers → **hand movements (HM)** → **perception of light (PL)** → **no perception of light (NoPL)** — so **PL is one step WORSE than HM**, and \"PL or worse\" is the small, sickest group at the bottom of the scale.\n\n`L18) Cataract II` slide 16 gives the treatment its split: *\"Tx: urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime). **Severe: vitrectomy.**\"* **This question is the definition of that word \"Severe\".** Everyone gets the tap and the intravitreal antibiotics (`opmcq-c12-3` keys the antibiotic as mandatory in all situations); **only the eye that has fallen to PL or worse goes straight to theatre for a vitrectomy**, which removes the infected gel and its bacterial load wholesale rather than injecting into it.\n\n*(That the threshold for immediate vitrectomy is set at light-perception-only vision, and that eyes above that threshold do as well with tap-and-inject alone, is **not taken from the course material** — the trial that established it returns zero case-insensitive hits across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 book chapter caches. What the module supplies is the acuity ladder, the *\"Severe: vitrectomy\"* rule, and endophthalmitis as an emergency; the numerical cut-off comes from outside.)*\n\n**Why each of the other four fails — three of them on the inequality alone:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **A. Admit all cases as it's an ocular emergency** | ⚠️ **True premise, wrong conclusion, and the most tempting distractor in the chapter.** Endophthalmitis *is* on the module's emergency list — `L1,2` slide 135 prints it beside CRAO, acute angle-closure and chemical burns — and this bank has already keyed **urgent referral** at `opmcq-c12-2`. But the question does not ask about admission alone; it asks for the indication to admit **and perform immediate vitrectomy**, and vitrectomy is what `L18` slide 16 reserves for *\"Severe\"* disease. Being an emergency is why everyone is treated at once; it is not why one subgroup is operated on. |\n| **B. HM or worse vision** | Sets the bar one rung too **high on the ladder**, sweeping in every hand-movements eye that the evidence says does as well without a vitrectomy — an unnecessary intraocular operation on an already inflamed eye. |\n| **C. PL or better vision** | The threshold at the right place and the inequality **inverted**: it operates on everyone *except* the group that needs it, and spares the eye at the bottom. |\n| **D. HM or better vision** | Wrong rung and wrong direction at once — it selects the eyes that are doing comparatively well. |\n\n**Read the ladder, not the letters:** three of these five options contain the correct abbreviation, and only one contains it pointing the correct way.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Every endophthalmitis gets a tap and intravitreal antibiotics; immediate vitrectomy is reserved for vision of PL or WORSE — the bottom of the acuity ladder, not the top.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.83 (Ch.12 Q15). The only question in the chapter keyed E' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-16', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'Which of the following is **NOT** true regarding **complications of posterior vitreous detachment**?',
  options:['Complications may include retinal tears / detachment','Complications are much more common in high hypermetropes','Retinal tears / detachment may cause vitreous hemorrhage','Complications are common following ocular trauma','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**The false statement is B, and it is false by one word: the risk belongs to high MYOPES, not high hypermetropes.** `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` prints it twice, in prose and in a table:\n\n> **Slide 6, rhegmatogenous retinal detachment** — *\"Common Causes: Aging (vitreous shrinkage), **high myopia** (nearsightedness), or eye trauma.\"*\n>\n> **Slide 7, the comparison table** — *Rhegmatogenous · Common Risk: **Myopia**, Aging.*\n\nThe reason is geometry: **the myopic eye is a long eye.** A globe stretched along its axis has a thinner, more stretched peripheral retina and an earlier, more mobile vitreous — so both the traction and the tissue it pulls on are worse. The hypermetropic eye is **short**, and the module attaches its characteristic disaster to the front of the eye instead: a shallow anterior chamber and a crowded angle, which is `L12`'s territory of angle-closure glaucoma, not the retina's.\n\n**⚠️ This is the module's single most reliable myopia/hypermetropia swap, and Grade Gain builds a question on the same misconception** — `opqb-t12-500` prints the hypermetrope claim as a **distractor** in a differently-framed question. The discriminating token here is the word **\"complications\"**, which is in this stem and not in that one; the shared item is one option, not one question.\n\n**Why the other three are TRUE — each is printed in the module:**\n\n| Option | Why it is true | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Complications may include retinal tears / detachment** | The book's definition of PVD carries its complication in the same sentence: *\"can predispose the patient to **retinal traction, retinal tears** and subsequent retinal detachment.\"* | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 |\n| **C. Retinal tears / detachment may cause vitreous hemorrhage** | A tear crosses retinal vessels; the blood goes into the gel. p.42 lists **vitreous haemorrhage** and **retinal tear / detachment** together as causes of floaters, and this bank keys both PVD and trauma as causes of vitreous haemorrhage at `opmcq-c12-18`. | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42; `opmcq-c12-18` |\n| **D. Complications are common following ocular trauma** | `L20` slide 6 names **\"eye trauma\"** as a common cause of rhegmatogenous detachment in the same breath as ageing and high myopia; `L25) Trauma I` runs the injury pathway in full. | `L20` sl.6 |\n| **E. None of the above** | Would assert all four are true; B is not. | |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'The complications of posterior vitreous detachment — tears, detachment, and the vitreous haemorrhage that follows a tear — cluster in HIGH MYOPES and after trauma; the long eye tears, the short eye closes its angle.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.83 (Ch.12 Q16). A negative-stem question; the key selects the one FALSE option' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-17', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
  stem:'Which of the following is **NOT** true regarding **endophthalmitis**?',
  options:["It's an ocular emergency",'It occurs most commonly due to endogenous spread of infection','It occurs most commonly postoperatively','Infectious organisms are most commonly bacterial','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"**B and C are the same claim pointing in opposite directions, so exactly one of them is the false one — and the module settles it decisively.** `L18) Cataract II` teaches endophthalmitis inside its list of **post-operative complications**, giving it a timeline measured from surgery (slide 16: *\"**24–72 hrs** (acute) or **weeks** (delayed)\"*) and an organism list drawn from the ocular surface (*\"CoNS (**Staphylococcus epidermidis** most common), S. aureus, streptococci\"*). Skin and lid-margin commensals reach the vitreous because an incision let them in. **That is the exogenous route, and the module treats it as the default.**\n\nThe endogenous route — organisms arriving through the bloodstream from a distant focus — is the rare one. The module touches it only obliquely: `L10) orbit` slide 27 lists **endophthalmitis** among the ocular complications of bacterial **orbital cellulitis**, an infection spreading from outside the globe rather than seeded through the circulation. *(That endogenous endophthalmitis is the minority route and clusters in intravenous drug users, indwelling lines, diabetics and the immunocompromised is **not taken from the course material**.)*\n\n**Why the other three are TRUE:**\n\n| Option | Why it is true | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. It's an ocular emergency** | `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 135 prints **Endophthalmitis** on its list of **ocular emergencies**, and `L18` slide 16 calls it the *\"Most serious vision-threatening complication\"* of cataract surgery. This bank keys the consequence — urgent referral — at `opmcq-c12-2`. | `L1,2` sl.135; `L18` sl.16 |\n| **C. It occurs most commonly postoperatively** | The whole of `L18` slide 16's account is post-operative: the timing runs from the operation, the risk factors are surgical, and the organisms are surface commensals. | `L18` sl.16 |\n| **D. Infectious organisms are most commonly bacterial** | Slide 16's causative list is bacterial without exception — coagulase-negative staphylococci, *S. aureus*, streptococci. Fungal endophthalmitis exists but the module attaches fungi to **trauma with plant matter** instead (`L15,16) Cornea`), which this bank keys at `opmcq-c12-19`. | `L18` sl.16; `L15,16` |\n| **E. None of the above** | Would assert all four are true; B is not. | |\n\n**⚠️ Note the internal consistency check the question offers for free:** B and C cannot both be true, so *\"None of the above\"* is impossible before any clinical knowledge is applied. The work is only in deciding which of the pair the module supports — and it supports C in the very deck that teaches the condition.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Endophthalmitis is most commonly EXOGENOUS — post-operative, bacterial, from ocular-surface commensals — and is an ocular emergency; the endogenous bloodborne route is the rare one.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf pp.83-84 (Ch.12 Q17). ⚠️ ORPHANED ACROSS THE PAGE BREAK: stem and all five options at the foot of p.83, the answer line at the head of p.84. A negative-stem question; the key selects the one FALSE option' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-18', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-acute',
  stem:'Which of the following can result in **vitreous hemorrhage**?',
  options:['PDR','PVD','RVO','Trauma','All of the above'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"**Every route to blood in the vitreous is a route to a vessel, and the four options are the four kinds of vessel accident this module teaches.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 heads its box of **acute painless loss of vision** with *\"1. Vitreous haemorrhage\"*, and `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 133 files **Vitreous Haemorrhage** in the *Acute* column of its loss-of-vision table — so the module treats it as a common endpoint rather than a single disease.\n\n| Option | The mechanism | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. PDR** — proliferative diabetic retinopathy | **New vessels**, graded by the deck as NVD and NVE, growing on the retinal surface and into the vitreous. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` heads its indications for vitreoretinal surgery with *\"**Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage**\"* — the deck records the bleed as the standard endpoint of proliferative disease. This bank keys the diabetic bleed at `opmcq-c12-4`. | `L19.1` |\n| **B. PVD** — posterior vitreous detachment | The separating gel **pulls**, and where it stays adherent it tears retina and the vessels crossing it. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17: *\"can predispose the patient to retinal traction, retinal tears and subsequent retinal detachment\"*; p.42 lists PVD and vitreous haemorrhage side by side among the causes of floaters. This bank builds `opmcq-c12-11` on exactly this — a PVD whose red reflex has gone. | `ophthalmology.pdf` pp.17, 42 |\n| **C. RVO** — retinal vein occlusion | A blocked vein raises pressure upstream until the retina bleeds, and chronic ischaemia drives **neovascularization**, which bleeds into the gel. `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` names *\"chronic macular oedema and neovascularization\"* among the complications of branch retinal vein occlusion. *(That the new vessels of an ischaemic RVO go on to produce vitreous haemorrhage is **not taken from the course material** — `L19.2`'s only \"vitreous\" hit is in its **sickle-cell** proliferative staging, not its RVO section.)* | `L19.2` |\n| **D. Trauma** | Direct rupture. `L25) Trauma I` runs both mechanisms — blunt injury tearing retinal vessels and penetrating injury opening the globe — and `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 6 names *\"eye trauma\"* among the common causes of the tears that bleed. This bank keys the traumatic route at `opmcq-c12-12` and `opmcq-c12-19`. | `L25`; `L20` sl.6 |\n\n**⚠️ Why \"All of the above\" is the answer and not a hedge.** These four are not four diseases that happen to share a symptom; they are the module's **complete differential for a bleed into the gel**, and the chapter has spent its length demonstrating each one. Grouping them is the point: the presentation — sudden painless loss with floaters and black shadows against the red reflex (`L3,4` slide 50) — is identical whichever vessel gave way, so the fundus tells you *that* it bled and the **history** tells you *why*.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-ret-dr` for the PDR route and `op-ret-vasc` for the RVO route. Filed under acute visual loss because that is where `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 and `L1,2` slide 133 file the haemorrhage itself.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Proliferative diabetic retinopathy, posterior vitreous detachment, retinal vein occlusion and trauma are the module\'s complete differential for vitreous haemorrhage — new vessels, traction, ischaemic neovascularization and direct rupture.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.84 (Ch.12 Q18). The source prints the abbreviations PDR, PVD and RVO unexpanded' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-19', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
  stem:'Which of the following is true regarding **infections resulting from penetrating eye trauma**?',
  options:['Gram positive bacteria are more common than gram negative ones','Retained intraocular foreign objects increase risk of infection with Bacillus species','Exposure to plant / vegetable matter suggests a fungal etiology','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"**All three statements hold, and the third is the one the module prints in its own words.**\n\n| Option | Verdict and grounding |\n|---|---|\n| **C. Plant / vegetable matter suggests a fungal etiology** | **The module's own rule**, from the cornea rather than the vitreous but the same principle. `L15,16) Cornea`, on the microbiology of microbial keratitis: *\"**Filamentous fungi (Aspergillus, Fusarium): trauma with plant matter.**\"* Filamentous fungi live on vegetation, so a branch, a thorn or a stalk delivers them directly through the wound. The organism is decided by **what did the injuring**, not by the eye. |\n| **A. Gram positive more common than gram negative** | True, and consistent with everything the module says about intraocular infection. `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 gives post-operative endophthalmitis a wholly Gram-positive causative list — *\"CoNS (Staphylococcus epidermidis most common), S. aureus, **streptococci**\"* — because the reservoir is the skin and lid margin, which is Gram-positive territory. *(The extension of that predominance specifically to post-traumatic endophthalmitis is **not taken from the course material**.)* |\n| **B. Retained intraocular foreign objects increase the risk of Bacillus infection** | True. *(**Not taken from the course material**, and measured: **\"Bacillus\" returns zero case-insensitive hits across all 29 cached ophthalmology decks and all 12 book chapter caches.** *Bacillus cereus* is a soil and vegetation organism carried in on metallic and organic foreign bodies, and it produces a fulminant, rapidly blinding endophthalmitis — which is why a **retained** foreign body is treated as an infection risk in its own right and not merely as a mechanical problem.)* What the module **does** print is the retained foreign body as a distinct entity demanding its own work-up: `L25) Trauma I` slide 52 requires a CT to *\"Determine the location and presence of a metallic IOFB\"* and forbids MRI — *\"**DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED**\"* — with slides 53/54 printing the CT and B-scan images. |\n| **E. None of the above** | Excluded; all three statements are true. |\n\n**⚠️ Why this question sits at the end of the chapter.** It closes the loop the chapter opened: `opmcq-c12-1` gave endophthalmitis arriving through a **surgical** incision, and this gives it arriving through a **traumatic** one. `opmcq-c12-17` establishes that the exogenous route dominates; this question says what comes in with it. The practical consequence — that a penetrating injury with organic contamination or a retained foreign body carries a far worse infective prognosis than a clean surgical wound — is why `L25) Trauma I` slide 51's open-globe instructions are so restrictive: *\"DO NOT PLACE A PATCH\"*, *\"DO NOT CHECK EYE PRESSURE\"*, shield the eye and refer.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-cat-surg`, which is where the module teaches endophthalmitis itself, at `L18` slide 16. Filed under trauma because the stem is entirely about the penetrating injury.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Post-traumatic intraocular infection is predicted by the injuring agent: Gram positives predominate overall, a retained foreign body raises the risk of Bacillus, and plant or vegetable matter points to filamentous fungi.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.84 (Ch.12 Q19). The source prints option C with spaces around the solidus, "plant / vegetable matter"' },

{ id:'opmcq-c12-20', bank:'house', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
  stem:'What is the appropriate management for an **uncomplicated posterior vitreous detachment** with **no retinal tears or hemorrhage**?',
  options:['Immediate laser therapy','Vitrectomy','No treatment','Intravitreal antibiotics'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"**The stem has already done the work: it states that the two complications have been excluded, and with them excluded there is nothing left to treat.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 defines the PVD as an **involutional** change — *\"With age the vitreous humour degenerates and condenses (vitreous syneresis) and may separate from the retina\"* — and gives it no drug and no operation. The separation is permanent and harmless; **the danger was never the detachment itself but what it might have torn on the way**, and the stem says it tore nothing.\n\nWhat replaces treatment is **instruction**. The patient is warned to return at once for a sudden shower of new floaters, an increase in flashes, or a curtain across the field, because those are the symptoms of the retinal tear or detachment that p.17 warns the PVD predisposes to and that `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` builds its account on.\n\n**Why each of the three treatments is wrong — each is the correct answer to a different question in this chapter:**\n\n| Option | What it actually treats | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Immediate laser therapy** | A **retinal break** — laser retinopexy seals the retina around a tear so subretinal fluid cannot track through it — and, in `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy`, the ischaemic retina of proliferative disease by panretinal photocoagulation. **The stem has explicitly excluded the tear**, so there is nothing to surround. |\n| **B. Vitrectomy** | The complicated vitreous: `L19.1`'s indications — *\"Severe **persistent** vitreous haemorrhage\"*, *\"Retinal detachment involving macula\"* — and, in `L18) Cataract II` slide 16, *\"**Severe**: vitrectomy\"* for endophthalmitis. This chapter keys it at `opmcq-c12-6` and sets its endophthalmitis threshold at `opmcq-c12-15`. Every one of those uses carries a severity qualifier this patient does not meet. |\n| **D. Intravitreal antibiotics** | **Endophthalmitis** — `L18` slide 16, *\"urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime)\"*; keyed at `opmcq-c12-3`. Nothing here is infected, and injecting a needle into a quiet eye risks creating the very infection the drug treats. |\n\n**⚠️ The chapter asks this judgement twice, and the discriminating token is in the stem, not the options.** `opmcq-c12-9` reaches the same verdict — *No specific treatment* — for the 22-year-old of `opmcq-c12-7`, but as a **back-reference** with a different option list (steroids, surgery, antibiotics, no specific treatment, none of the above) and with the absence of complications **inherited from the chain**. This question is **free-standing** and states *\"with no retinal tears or hemorrhage\"* in its own words. Same principle, two constructions.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology MCQ.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:'Once a retinal tear and a vitreous haemorrhage have been excluded, a posterior vitreous detachment needs no laser, no vitrectomy and no antibiotic — only warning symptoms to return on.',
  source:'ophthalmology MCQ.pdf p.84 (Ch.12 Q20, the last question of the chapter; the lower half of p.84 is blank and p.85 opens the "13. The retina" banner). Four options only — the source prints no E' }
